      "id": 8,
      "options": {
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "---\n## 📖 How to Add a Template Variable to This Dashboard\n\nRight now every panel title and query is hardcoded to **server-prod-01**. The steps below walk you through creating a <code>&#36;server</code> variable so any viewer can pick the server they care about from a dropdown.\n\n⏱️ **Total time: ~3 minutes** · Tip: open this dashboard in a second tab so you can read the steps while editing.\n\n> **Why bother?** Template variables let one dashboard serve many servers, namespaces, or environments — without duplicating panels.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "pluginVersion": "13.3.0-36978054224",
      "targets": [],
      "title": "Step-by-Step: Adding Template Variables",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 28
      },
      "id": 9,
      "options": {
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "### ① Open the Variable Editor\n\n1. Click the **⋮ (kebab / three-dot) menu** at the top-right of the dashboard toolbar.\n2. Select **Dashboard settings** (or press `d` then `s`).\n3. In the left sidebar, choose **Variables**.\n4. Click the blue **+ Add variable** button.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "pluginVersion": "13.3.0-36978054224",
      "targets": [],
      "title": "Step 1 – Open Variable Settings",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 36
      },
      "id": 10,
      "options": {
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "### ② Configure the Variable (≈1 min)\n\nPick **Custom** as the type, then copy/paste each value:\n\n| Field | Value to paste |\n|---|---|\n| **Name** | <code>server</code> |\n| **Label** | <code>Server</code> |\n| **Custom options** | <code>server-prod-01,server-prod-02,server-staging-01</code> |\n\n✅ Check the **Preview of values** shows 3 servers, then click **Back to dashboard**.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "pluginVersion": "13.3.0-36978054224",
      "targets": [],
      "title": "Step 2 – Configure the Variable",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 36
      },
      "id": 11,
      "options": {
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "### ③ Use the Variable in a Panel Title (≈30 sec)\n\n1. Hover the **CPU Usage – server-prod-01** panel and press <code>e</code> (or ⋮ menu → **Edit**).\n2. In **Title**, replace <code>server-prod-01</code> with <code>&#36;server</code>.\n3. Click **Back to dashboard**.\n\n✅ The title now shows whatever is picked in the **Server** dropdown.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "pluginVersion": "13.3.0-36978054224",
      "targets": [],
      "title": "Step 3 – Use the Variable in a Panel Title",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 44
      },
      "id": 12,
      "options": {
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "### ④ Use the Variable in a Query (≈30 sec)\n\n1. Edit the same CPU panel again.\n2. In the query editor, set **Alias** to <code>&#36;server</code>.\n3. Click **Back to dashboard** — the legend now follows the dropdown.\n\n💡 With a real datasource like Prometheus, the same idea is a label filter:\n<pre>rate(http_requests_total{instance=\"&#36;server\"}[&#36;__rate_interval])</pre>\n\n⭐ **Bonus:** repeat steps ③–④ on the other panels.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "pluginVersion": "13.3.0-36978054224",
      "targets": [],
      "title": "Step 4 – Use the Variable in a Query",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 44
      },
      "id": 13,
      "options": {
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "### ⑤ Verify the Dropdown Appears\n\n1. **Save** the dashboard (`Ctrl+S` / `Cmd+S`).\n2. You should now see a **Server** dropdown at the top of the dashboard.\n3. Switch between values — all panels with <code>&#36;server</code> in their title or query will update automatically.\n\n🎉 You’ve added your first template variable!",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "pluginVersion": "13.3.0-36978054224",
      "targets": [],
      "title": "Step 5 – Verify the Dropdown",
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 52
      },
      "id": 14,
      "options": {
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "### 🚀 Going Further\n\n| Variable Type | Best Used For |\n|---|---|\n| **Custom** | Static lists (environments, regions) |\n| **Query** | Dynamic lists from your datasource (pods, hosts) |\n| **Datasource** | Switch the data source on the fly |\n| **Interval** | Let users pick time bucket size |\n| **Ad hoc filters** | Free-form label filtering (Prometheus/Loki) |\n\nVariables can also **chain** — e.g. `$namespace` filters the options available in `$pod`.\n\n📖 [Full docs: grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/dashboards/variables/](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/dashboards/variables/)",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "pluginVersion": "13.3.0-36978054224",
      "targets": [],
      "title": "Going Further – Variable Types",
      "type": "text"
    }
